"relationship": "same_topic",
"comparison": "Goldman Sachs and JPMorgan Chase share nearly identical language regarding the adverse effects of rating reductions on liquidity and funding costs. JPMorgan states that 'Reductions in JPMorganChase's credit ratings may adversely affect its liquidity and cost of funding' [jpm_2024_1A_P100_S001]. Similarly, Goldman Sachs discloses that 'Reductions in our credit ratings or an increase in our credit spreads may adversely affect our liquidity and cost of funding' [gs_2024_1A_P048_S001]. Both companies also explicitly link rating reductions to increased borrowing costs and potential contract triggers (collateral/termination), with Goldman Sachs specifying that 'counterparties could be permitted to terminate contracts with us or require us to post additional collateral' [gs_2024_1A_P049_S003].",
